eld-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,x(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} |  |  | $\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-4}} |  | $\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite our system, highlighting the coefficients:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+0\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=(-1)\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c856bc-0d2d-5ba0-b0e1-3078748c4516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357103} {:answer/id #uuid "48b0d13c-226d-546b-80ae-08ae3855c086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357104} {:answer/id #uuid "369d35b5-3eee-5218-a71c-59f4ffdf6da6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357105} {:answer/id #uuid "4e1d3587-f223-5e62-9f09-b225f94d545e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357106} {:answer/id #uuid "687e8bf1-9baf-503e-aba5-b5ce8d1fe28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "687e8bf1-9baf-503e-aba5-b5ce8d1fe28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357107} :answer-field/id #uuid "761a13fe-f1e4-57ea-a134-c6a086ae4d26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6610a46-90b9-5a9e-9cf1-d65ca11337a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332595" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]){e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcbcf26d-9a3d-5429-b765-2ae696428433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357110} {:answer/id #uuid "63cc2cf3-b52c-52de-a1fd-ca67e70c18ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357111} {:answer/id #uuid "721b6975-7403-5450-a00e-d05e4d46e2f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357112} {:answer/id #uuid "a2da0cac-758b-50fc-a8c7-3fe6379a49b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{5t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357113} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae03b40-a28f-522c-bc68-801d33987e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae03b40-a28f-522c-bc68-801d33987e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357114} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd145619-dacc-5741-86c7-2dc608bc3865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e0bd9ec-c740-533c-8086-569db6f2cda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332596" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is sinusoidal. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548acc87-db4c-5b02-8b6e-859fbcf8dc2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357117} {:answer/id #uuid "1ad09de0-0ab9-550d-8f2b-607f55cf933d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357118} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb5083d-b1a1-57f2-9fc8-3d40c5525453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357119} {:answer/id #uuid "3e1b0d46-06a9-5acd-a70e-7b7856ef3786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357120} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5c9956-239d-5d9e-b338-7c77779f4b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad09de0-0ab9-550d-8f2b-607f55cf933d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357118} :answer-field/id #uuid "429748dd-993b-5e10-84a3-e6315afee3e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b74c0fe3-3f12-5992-93c1-45a8876eedf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332739" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution has the same exponential factor, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa7c985-6585-5f20-bc53-3770fcf37d21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357124} {:answer/id #uuid "6ea8a42e-16aa-5bb2-9901-9c52516de809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357125} {:answer/id #uuid "bb228741-d103-5d3d-83df-735db3d82f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357126} {:answer/id #uuid "a7911fc9-4cf9-5372-8b89-e7834866453d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}(t[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357127} {:answer/id #uuid "178350d6-29c5-5f43-b816-306bd01b271a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ea8a42e-16aa-5bb2-9901-9c52516de809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357125} :answer-field/id #uuid "4efc2dd6-7963-5314-88d5-e6f3450efaeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "186cef9a-6614-5640-8b27-9dd6ea46c5a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332749" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]){e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eecbfc4-f7eb-5d4c-bea6-8fd21839804c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Form of a Particular Solution for Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80ae5d85-af3f-598b-b33f-298228417554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357131} {:answer/id #uuid "9a81c2de-ba2c-5828-97c8-717ae027f40e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357132} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbd7152-ee8c-54bc-b5c8-aef917ab682b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357133} {:answer/id #uuid "20952b92-5c71-5f6d-b61e-189d36078e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-t}(t[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}])" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357134} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc2af10-15af-5819-b390-8781fec7eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc2af10-15af-5819-b390-8781fec7eafa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357135} :answer-field/id #uuid "8255c24c-93a7-5d8b-a7e7-6c8953665d4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8d1d647-1c74-582a-9da6-b6c8499bc05b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332805" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+2t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+2t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial in $t$ of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2$, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={t}^{2}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df38670-79ff-542c-8fa9-8108579ceb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df38670-79ff-542c-8fa9-8108579ceb3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357138} :answer-field/id #uuid "896a85ec-7090-5631-a11f-1ecbb2808bd8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357137} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2277edd-9833-5909-beb5-38019e7660aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2277edd-9833-5909-beb5-38019e7660aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357140} :answer-field/id #uuid "301ccb7f-be02-5837-99af-beac99a6bae9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be07f89-dd85-5fa8-8bbc-ce3c67c8162c" :question/math-academy-id "q-332866" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{7}{10}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3b \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-a \\\\ -b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3b+4 \\\\ 3a+1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3b+4=-a \\\\ 3a+1=-b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\na=3b-4.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n3(3b-4)+1=-b\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{11}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\na=3\\cdot \\frac{11}{10}-4=\\frac{33}{10}-\\frac{40}{10}=-\\frac{7}{10}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{10} \\\\ \\frac{11}{10}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff0e34b9-7362-53e9-9c8b-93086309eb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357143} {:answer/id #uuid "849993e6-5b06-553e-b861-9a83bf1212a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff0e34b9-7362-53e9-9c8b-93086309eb72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357143} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5198c92-325d-5fd9-a321-efb3d77244e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357142} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79528b3a-a1e1-52d6-a5fc-be24a67ff4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79528b3a-a1e1-52d6-a5fc-be24a67ff4d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357146} :answer-field/id #uuid "d36c2e20-01fd-582c-a0e5-f1d200eabe38" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357145}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3416958e-ba31-5e6d-b1f6-b34051c051b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332914" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -4[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a \\\\ 5b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a-14 \\\\ 5b-27\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a-14=-4a \\\\ 5b-27=-4b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3a-14=-4a\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5b-27=-4b\\,⇒\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b639e3-0dae-5f72-8ff3-1ca93324ad9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44b639e3-0dae-5f72-8ff3-1ca93324ad9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357149} :answer-field/id #uuid "56eba36d-a5e4-5b18-bec8-9edc4bc807ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357148} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e93b9543-2a76-5975-883b-f51866952c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e93b9543-2a76-5975-883b-f51866952c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357151} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c6dd408-7a05-57f2-b738-be7bd0183a86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cde0fef1-c854-5a57-a53c-75ef5c5675fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-333007" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t+1 \\\\ 8t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({a}_{2}-2)t+({b}_{2}+1) \\\\ (-2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8)t+(-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}-2=0 \\\\ -2{a}_{1}-3{a}_{2}+8=0 \\\\ {b}_{2}+1={a}_{1} \\\\ -2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=2,\\,{a}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=0,\\,{b}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17afdaa3-a7dd-5051-9224-2e344490a499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{41}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357154} {:answer/id #uuid "e215021d-0323-564a-9c9a-e8cb04185770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17afdaa3-a7dd-5051-9224-2e344490a499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{41}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357154} :answer-field/id #uuid "c36c61c7-d962-593d-8886-db8607aa4bc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357153} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5e6965-ccf7-5a69-9370-31005e43e0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a5e6965-ccf7-5a69-9370-31005e43e0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357157} :answer-field/id #uuid "079be188-24e1-5d1c-b4df-3122a2f63619" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bda8201-0048-53f2-ac65-1c8a54a619d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-333008" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $\\frac{11}{6}$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ 3{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3t+4 \\\\ 2t-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3)t+(2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4) \\\\ (3{a}_{2}+2)t+(3{b}_{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-3=0 \\\\ 3{a}_{2}+2=0 \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}+4={a}_{1} \\\\ 3{b}_{2}-1={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe first two equations, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,{a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{9},\\,{b}_{1}=-\\frac{41}{36}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{41}{36} \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dddc6159-ac4a-50ac-a366-08a4b809c925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e5db5a-90a4-5a4f-9fdf-ce5d0f3a189d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08e5db5a-90a4-5a4f-9fdf-ce5d0f3a189d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357160} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82350c2-63ce-5e91-87b1-037b474de98d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5c16a6-bf3b-5fe9-b0f3-f57d0071e5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c16a6-bf3b-5fe9-b0f3-f57d0071e5e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357162} :answer-field/id #uuid "29bd9965-3de3-5a6c-8847-ca86d3026b88" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20e4be9b-6e05-53c0-97df-25183753bdb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-333010" :question/problem "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$ |  | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ |  | $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}t+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{b}_{1},{b}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1/2 & 3/2 \\\\ 3/2 & -1/2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})+\\frac{3}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({a}_{1}t+{b}_{1})-\\frac{1}{2}({a}_{2}t+{b}_{2})\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t-3 \\\\ 2t+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0t+{a}_{1} \\\\ 0t+{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1)t+(-\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3) \\\\ (\\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2)t+(\\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{a}_{2}}{2}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{3{a}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}}{2}+2=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{b}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{3{b}_{2}}{2}-3={a}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{3{b}_{1}}{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}}{2}+1={a}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first two equations, we get \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=-\\frac{7}{4},\\,{a}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting these into the third and fourth equations, and solving for ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=-\\frac{11}{8},\\,{b}_{2}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{11}{8} \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3a23777-1510-5482-ad07-25cdc7cf7432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a23777-1510-5482-ad07-25cdc7cf7432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357165} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e340fb7-d37a-54eb-b6fe-cf908839d285" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357164} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183dcaf5-f211-51e0-9fee-6462bceee52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183dcaf5-f211-51e0-9fee-6462bceee52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357167} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc3f026-3e0a-50cb-86c6-7900411e09d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f4c56bc4-840a-5435-8259-6308509aa69b" :question/math-academy-id "q-333595" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3(0)}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-0}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=3 \\\\ {c}_{2}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=2.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "089fc913-a434-57ae-ac9f-2d5913c1497b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "089fc913-a434-57ae-ac9f-2d5913c1497b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357170} :answer-field/id #uuid "128c36b6-3fb7-53bc-bebc-2e06ab2d062f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7bed68f-897f-5444-a2e4-188c643eeef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7bed68f-897f-5444-a2e4-188c643eeef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357172} :answer-field/id #uuid "5db70c38-a41a-5ca3-819c-af52c7eaf465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4dcc3ea-f856-5a47-9560-11e154e52e77" :question/math-academy-id "q-333611" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}-2t+2 \\\\ -3{t}^{2}+5t-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(0)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3(0)}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-(0)^{2}+2 \\\\ (0)^{2}-0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2 \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+2=8 \\\\ {c}_{2}=-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=6.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-7.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-7\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c08ea69-d254-5c2b-9ec2-7a1bdedfc9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357175} {:answer/id #uuid "614a60f2-e1ea-547a-bfb9-863c3a2272a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c08ea69-d254-5c2b-9ec2-7a1bdedfc9ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357175} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2e197e-43e9-5932-92d7-40c5868b52b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34940ec7-16e8-58c9-9848-1a0009cb5624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34940ec7-16e8-58c9-9848-1a0009cb5624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357178} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5df099e-c56d-5846-9041-daff0ef042e6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5372ae8-979a-5cf5-b0e2-1e35dacacda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-333615" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-2t+6 \\\\ -2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2\\cdot 0+3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1 \\\\ {c}_{2}+3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-1=10 \\\\ {c}_{2}+3=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=11.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=11\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-1 \\\\ -2t+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3226ca-dc52-57ef-a59e-baf05aba8cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357181} {:answer/id #uuid "a9180497-488c-55a8-937e-f9303372d604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3226ca-dc52-57ef-a59e-baf05aba8cdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357181} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e8195b-c669-5f24-af46-0f9f7df49f4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357180} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8ca6371-0335-548a-ba1c-51d11989cff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ca6371-0335-548a-ba1c-51d11989cff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357184} :answer-field/id #uuid "c377f4e0-9b04-5830-a356-d9990abf4bd7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9183adc5-0794-5355-9c33-e7bfc7ff5220" :question/math-academy-id "q-333631" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{-2(0)}\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}(2\\cdot 0-1){e}^{-2(0)} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{-2(0)}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+1=-1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$  Substituting this into the second equation and solving for ${c}_{2},$ we get ${c}_{2}=8.$ \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+8\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}(2t+1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ (t+1){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d5ab3ac-770b-58c9-b30f-659773ea1a20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem for an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f071e6d6-f421-529e-a56d-c699836d6661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f071e6d6-f421-529e-a56d-c699836d6661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357187} :answer-field/id #uuid "80b2d03e-2a3a-5409-a818-d9f4cff871ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357186} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9c1df9-b5b5-5915-86b2-505441361077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d9c1df9-b5b5-5915-86b2-505441361077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357189} :answer-field/id #uuid "defe59f2-a3a6-5a8b-a102-827d8b26e083" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4730bc91-31e3-5f1b-84cf-5a82c844c3cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-333638" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=4 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}]}
